In the News
Executive View
The first large cardiovascular-outcomes test of Lp(a) lowering failed.
Pelacarsen reduced the biomarker but not cardiovascular events, weakening con=
fidence that Lp(a) reduction alone will translate into benefit and materially=
 raising the evidentiary bar for Amgen=E2=80=99s olpasiran and Lilly=E2=80=99=
s lepodisiran. (
novartis.com
)
Fatal inflammatory events reset the risk assessment for autoimmune CAR-T.
Novartis paused eight rap-cel studies after three deaths from complications o=
f IEC-HS; Bristol Myers Squibb separately paused zola-cel enrollment followin=
g inflammatory events. (
lupus.org
)
FDA institutionalized treatment switching before radiographic progression.
Camizestrant became the first cancer therapy approved for use when serial cir=
culating-tumor-DNA testing detects an acquired resistance mutation before ima=
ging shows progression. (
fda.gov
)
Two bispecifics strengthened the case that implementation can be as different=
iating as efficacy.
AbbVie=E2=80=99s monthly etentamig delivered strong pivotal myeloma results w=
ith predominantly low-grade cytokine-release syndrome, while ivonescimab achi=
eved an overall-survival advantage over Keytruda in a China-only lung-cancer =
study. (
news.abbvie.com
)
Neurological antisense therapy produced a sharp success/failure contrast.
Ionis won the first Alexander-disease approval as Ultragenyx=E2=80=99s Angelm=
an program failed unequivocally in Phase 3, reinforcing that target engagemen=
t and encouraging uncontrolled data cannot substitute for reproducible functi=
onal benefit. (
fda.gov
)
Strategy Narrative
Pelacarsen turns Lp(a) from a biomarker race into an outcomes problem
What changed
Novartis reported on September 4 that the 8,323-patient Phase 3 Lp(a)HORIZON =
trial failed its primary four-component cardiovascular endpoint despite lower=
ing Lp(a). Participants had established cardiovascular disease and were alrea=
dy receiving guideline-directed lipid-lowering and antihypertensive therapy. =
Effect sizes, achieved Lp(a) reductions and component endpoints remain undisc=
losed. (
novartis.com
)
What it tells us
This does not conclusively refute Lp(a) biology: pelacarsen=E2=80=99s degree,=
 consistency and duration of reduction may matter, as may baseline Lp(a), res=
idual risk and endpoint composition. It does, however, refute the assumption =
that lowering the biomarker with any effective oligonucleotide will necessari=
ly reduce events.
Commercial expectations for the entire category should therefore be rebased a=
round outcomes rather than biomarker potency. Amgen and Lilly are testing siR=
NAs capable of deeper, more durable Lp(a) suppression, but their ongoing Phas=
e 3 programs now carry more biological=E2=80=94not merely execution=E2=80=94r=
isk. (
investors.amgen.com
)
What to watch next
The full Lp(a)HORIZON data, particularly achieved reduction, treatment durati=
on, high-Lp(a) subgroups and individual cardiovascular-event components.
Autoimmune CAR-T=E2=80=99s benefit-risk threshold moved materially higher
What changed
Novartis halted screening, randomization and dosing across eight autoimmune a=
nd neurological rapcabtagene-autoleucel studies after three participants deve=
loped serious immune effector cell-associated hemophagocytic syndrome and sub=
sequently died. Bristol Myers separately paused autoimmune studies of zolacab=
tagene autoleucel after detecting inflammatory events described as transient =
and reversible. (
lupus.org
)
What it tells us
The therapeutic thesis=E2=80=94deep B-cell depletion producing durable, poten=
tially drug-free remission=E2=80=94remains compelling. But severe autoimmune =
disease generally offers less tolerance for treatment-related mortality than =
refractory cancer. A safety profile acceptable in oncology may therefore be c=
ommercially and regulatorily unacceptable in lupus, multiple sclerosis or mya=
sthenia gravis.
These events do not establish a class-wide toxicity. They will nevertheless i=
ncrease scrutiny of cell-expansion kinetics, dose, lymphodepletion, patient s=
election, inflammatory biomarkers and whether rheumatology or neurology sites=
 can recognize and manage delayed immune complications.
What to watch next
Whether the events cluster by indication, protocol or manufacturing design; r=
egulatory requirements for restarting the studies; and whether competing prog=
rams alter dosing or monitoring proactively.
Camizestrant makes serial liquid biopsy part of treatment=E2=80=94not merely =
diagnosis
What changed
FDA granted accelerated approval to AstraZeneca=E2=80=99s oral SERD camizestr=
ant with a continuing CDK4/6 inhibitor when Guardant360 CDx detects an emergi=
ng ESR1 mutation during aromatase-inhibitor/CDK4/6 therapy, before radiograph=
ic progression. In the 315-patient SERENA-6 trial, median progression-free su=
rvival was 16.0 months versus 9.2 months for continued aromatase inhibition, =
a 56% reduction in progression or death risk. (
fda.gov
)
What it tells us
The strategic advance is broader than another endocrine drug. FDA has endorse=
d longitudinal molecular surveillance as a trigger for changing treatment whi=
le the patient remains clinically and radiographically stable. That creates r=
ecurring diagnostic demand and could extend first-line CDK4/6 use while displ=
acing some post-progression therapy.
The unresolved question is whether earlier switching improves survival or pri=
marily shifts the treatment clock forward, exposing patients to a new drug=E2=
=80=94and payers to additional cost=E2=80=94sooner.
What to watch next
Overall-survival maturation, confirmatory requirements, adherence to serial c=
tDNA testing and payer policies governing testing frequency.
Etentamig and ivonescimab pressure incumbents through different forms of diff=
erentiation
AbbVie=E2=80=99s Phase 3 CERVINO trial showed a 74.0% response rate for BCMA=
=C3=97CD3 bispecific etentamig versus 45.7% with standard regimens, with a PF=
S hazard ratio of 0.40. Grade 3/4 infections remained substantial at 27.7%, b=
ut cytokine-release syndrome was predominantly grade 1, with no grade 3 or hi=
gher events reported. Monthly dosing began after a single step-up dose. (
news.abbvie.com
)
The value proposition is therefore not simply another active BCMA therapy. A =
manageable initiation process and monthly schedule could move treatment beyon=
d specialized centers, expanding the economically reachable market even witho=
ut demonstrating superior efficacy to another BCMA bispecific.
Separately, ivonescimab monotherapy achieved statistically significant overal=
l survival versus pembrolizumab in PD-L1-positive first-line NSCLC in China. =
Coming one week after its biliary-tract survival win, the result further stre=
ngthens the PD-1/VEGF architecture; however, undisclosed effect sizes and the=
 China-only population leave global differentiation unresolved. Summit=E2=80=
=99s U.S. decision for a different EGFR-mutant indication is due November 14.=
 (
smmttx.com
)
Additional Strategic Signals
Rare neurological ASOs diverged on functional evidence.
FDA approved Ionis=E2=80=99s quarterly intrathecal zilganersen after a 49-pat=
ient controlled study demonstrated improved walking speed at 61 weeks in elig=
ible patients with Alexander disease. By contrast, Ultragenyx found no suppor=
tive efficacy signal on cognition, its multidomain responder index or any com=
ponent measure in Phase 3 Angelman syndrome. The Ionis opportunity is commerc=
ially constrained by extreme rarity, but scientifically validates GFAP reduct=
ion and Ionis=E2=80=99s independent-launch model. (
fda.gov
)
Voluntary MFN agreements are becoming negotiated policy safe harbors.
Nine additional manufacturers joined the administration=E2=80=99s pricing fra=
mework, extending agreements to companies representing a reported 89% of the =
branded market. Commitments include Medicaid MFN access, future-launch pricin=
g and $19.6 billion in collective U.S. manufacturing investment. BridgeBio ex=
pects orphan-only products to be excluded and anticipates protection from fut=
ure pricing mandates, although its SEC filing explicitly cautions that those =
interpretations may change. (
whitehouse.gov
)
Medtronic=E2=80=99s earnings altered the robotics thesis more than the financ=
ial beat did.
The company paired strong cardiac-ablation growth with a new Cornerstone Robo=
tics partnership and FDA clearance of its next-generation Touch Surgery Aide =
platform. Its 13.7% quarterly organic growth should not be extrapolated=E2=80=
=94the extra selling week contributed approximately $570 million=E2=80=94but =
Medtronic is assembling a broader robotics, AI and instrumentation portfolio =
rather than relying solely on Hugo. (
news.medtronic.com
)
What I=E2=80=99m Watching Next
Full Lp(a)HORIZON data and responses from competing siRNA developers.
Regulatory attribution and restart conditions for autoimmune CAR-T studies.
Overall-survival evidence supporting camizestrant=E2=80=99s pre-progression t=
reatment switch.
Complete HARMONi-2 and etentamig safety, efficacy and administration data.
Ultragenyx=E2=80=99s disposition of apazunersen and resulting expense reducti=
ons.
Bottom Line
This week challenged the assumption that compelling biology reliably becomes =
clinical value. Lp(a) lowering failed its first major outcomes test; an Angel=
man ASO failed despite promising earlier evidence; and autoimmune CAR-T encou=
ntered a potentially modality-defining safety problem. The counterexamples we=
re equally instructive: camizestrant coupled molecular surveillance to a larg=
e PFS gain, etentamig combined efficacy with deployability, and zilganersen c=
onverted target reduction into measurable function.
The strategic premium is shifting toward therapies that prove not only that t=
hey engage the intended biology, but that doing so improves outcomes safely, =
reproducibly and within a workable care pathway.
Recent Earnings Highlights
Medtronic (
MDT
)
+15.3% Last 3m
One of the largest medical-device companies, Medtronic develops and manufactu=
res therapeutic medical devices for chronic diseases. Its portfolio includes =
pacemakers, defibrillators, transcatheter heart valves, stents, spinal fixati=
on devices, neurovascular products, advanced energy, ablation laser therapy, =
and surgical tools. The company primarily markets its products to healthcare =
institutions and physicians in the United States, Western Europe, and Japan. =
Foreign sales account for roughly 50% of the company's total sales.
Reported:
September 1, 2026 =C2=B7
Google Finance earnings page
Earnings Call Summary
Medtronic delivered a strong start to fiscal 2027, reporting Q1 revenue of $9=
.8 billion and adjusted EPS of $1.45, both exceeding company expectations. Or=
ganic revenue growth reached 13.7%, boosted by approximately 670 basis points=
 from an extra selling week. Major segments including CRM (+15%), CST (+13%),=
 and Surgical (+9%) all showed strong underlying market demand.
At a Glance
Medtronic Beats Q1 Earnings Estimates:
Medtronic reported a quarterly revenue of $9.756 billion and an adjusted EPS =
of $1.14, outperforming the estimated revenue of $9.545 billion.
Strong Cardiovascular and Portfolio Performance:
The company delivered 13.7% organic revenue growth, heavily supported by an 8=
8% surge in its Cardiac Ablation Solutions business and broad strength across=
 major segments.
Full Year Financial Guidance Raised:
Following robust quarterly performance, management raised its fiscal year 202=
7 organic revenue growth guidance to 7.25%=E2=80=937.75% and boosted adjusted=
 EPS guidance to a range of $5.94 to $6.00.
Expanded Footprint in Robotic Surgery:
Medtronic disclosed a $700 million strategic partnership and distribution agr=
eement with Cornerstone Robotics for its Sentire surgical system in select in=
ternational markets.
Extra Selling Week Drives Growth:
The headline financial performance for the first quarter included an extra fi=
scal selling week, which contributed approximately $570 million to the overal=
l organic revenue figures.
